On Apr 29, 3:54 pm, Nathan <ntsp...@netscape.net> wrote:
> Why? What is the problem you are trying to solve here?

I would not call it a problem in the sense that it is something that
is currently broken and needs to be fixed. I gave a couple of reasons
for automated skill advancement in the rationale.

> >If I remember correctly, right now the only way to
> >lose a skill level in a skill - so that you can reassign it to
> >another skill - is to lose the experience level where that
> >skill point was spent.
>
> Regretting using up some? That's an intentional effect.

Is the intention really to make players regret their choices? Then my
intention is to make players stick with their choices for a while, not
forever.

> >   - Automatically use up all currently available skill points.
>
> I don't like this. You need a better reason to reduce the available
> options.

If the skill system is fully automated, then does it not follow that
it should make efficient use of the available resources?

> >   - Automatically redistribute skill points so that frequently and
> >     recently used skills tend to have Expert level, while infrequently
> >     and not recently used skills tend to have Basic level.
>
> What if I like casting Attack spells, but I don't want the Skilled/
> Expert
> effects of cone of cold and fireball?

If a spell become worse as the player advances in skill, I would
consider that a problem with that particular spell, not the skill
system.

> >   - Periodically decay the advance counts for skills so that
> >     advance points earned more recently matter more. For
> >     instance, decay all advance counts to 90% of their respective
> >     values every N turns. (Could be done after adjusting stats for
> >     exercise/abuse.)
>
> It already takes a long long time to earn those points. Let's not turn
> NetHack into one of those games where you just grind a lot before
> getting on with playing your game.

Agreed. I do not see this as an argument against automated skill
advancement, though.

